Subject: src: on OpenVMS, run the output filter chain without fork()

flex sends its output through a chain of processes connected by pipes:
tee_header (a forked copy of flex) splits off the header, m4 expands each
branch, and fix_linedirs (another forked flex) corrects the #line
directives.  OpenVMS has no fork().  Every stage reads all its input
before it ends, so on VMS the stages run one after another over temporary
files in SYS$SCRATCH once flex has finished writing:

- filter_apply_chain() diverts flex's output to a file and keeps a
  duplicate of the real output descriptor (the -o file, or stdout);
- filter_vms_finish(), called from flex_main()'s exit path in place of
  the wait() loop, runs tee_header, then for each branch m4 (started with
  vfork()/execv(), standard input and output redirected; a failed execv()
  returns in flex, so it is reported, not _exit()ed) and fix_linedirs.

The text of tee_header and the work of fix_linedirs move into
tee_header_streams() and fix_linedirs_streams(), which the Unix filters
call on stdin/stdout as before.  m4 defaults to the M4 kit,
M4$ROOT:[BIN]M4.EXE (the M4 logical name overrides it), and main() exits
through vms_exit() so that a failed run has error severity under DCL.

